GCD organizes work into **dispatch queues**, each either **serial** (one task at a time, in order) or **concurrent** (multiple tasks may run simultaneously, order not guaranteed). The **main queue** is a special serial queue tied to the main thread — it's where UIKit and SwiftUI expect all rendering and layout work to happen, and it's serial specifically so UI updates never race with each other.

```swift
DispatchQueue.global(qos: .userInitiated).async {
    let result = expensiveComputation()
    DispatchQueue.main.async {
        self.label.text = result   // back on main before touching UI
    }
}
```

The pattern above — do work off main, hop back to main to update UI — is the single most repeated shape in pre-structured-concurrency iOS code, and getting the hop back wrong is the most common concurrency bug on the platform.

Before structured concurrency, chaining three dependent async calls meant three nested completion handler closures — the infamous "pyramid of doom" — each with its own error-handling branch.

```swift
// Before: nested completion handlers
fetchUser { user in
    fetchOrders(for: user) { orders in
        fetchDetails(for: orders) { details in
            // three levels deep, three separate error paths
        }
    }
}

// After: async/await, same behavior, linear code
let user = try await fetchUser()
let orders = try await fetchOrders(for: user)
let details = try await fetchDetails(for: orders)
```

The `async` version isn't just prettier — it's a single, ordinary `try`/`catch` for all three calls, and the compiler enforces that you can't forget to call the continuation (a real, recurring bug with completion-handler closures).

```swift
@MainActor
class ProfileViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var name = ""

    func loadProfile(id: String) async {
        do {
            let profile = try await api.fetchProfile(id)
            self.name = profile.name   // already on main actor, safe
        } catch {
            self.name = "Error loading profile"
        }
    }
}
```

Marking the whole class `@MainActor` means every property access and method call on it is guaranteed to run on the main actor unless explicitly opted out — so the UI mutation after `await` needs no manual `DispatchQueue.main.async` hop. The `await` point is where this function can suspend and let other work run; execution resumes back on the main actor automatically once `fetchProfile` completes.

An **actor** is a reference type, like a class, but the compiler enforces that its mutable state can only be touched by one task at a time — accessing an actor's state from outside requires `await`, and the actor serializes those accesses instead of letting them race.

```swift
actor ImageCache {
    private var cache: [URL: UIImage] = [:]

    func image(for url: URL) -> UIImage? { cache[url] }
    func store(_ image: UIImage, for url: URL) { cache[url] = image }
}
```

Two tasks calling `store` concurrently on the same `ImageCache` can't corrupt `cache` the way two threads mutating a plain dictionary could — the actor queues the accesses. This is Swift's answer to the exact class of data races that used to require careful manual locking (`NSLock`, a serial dispatch queue used purely as a mutex) with no compiler help catching a missed lock.

A `Task` starts a new unit of asynchronous work; a `TaskGroup` runs several child tasks concurrently and structurally ties their lifetime to the group — if the group scope exits (an error, the parent task cancelled), Swift automatically cancels any still-running children rather than leaving them orphaned.

```swift
func loadAllThumbnails(urls: [URL]) async throws -> [UIImage] {
    try await withThrowingTaskGroup(of: UIImage.self) { group in
        for url in urls {
            group.addTask { try await downloadThumbnail(url) }
        }
        var images: [UIImage] = []
        for try await image in group {
            images.append(image)
        }
        return images
    }
}
```

That automatic cancellation propagation is the **structured** part of structured concurrency — a plain GCD dispatch group has no equivalent; cancelling in-flight work there means manually tracking and checking a flag inside every closure yourself.

`async` functions can't be called from ordinary synchronous code directly — a button's `@IBAction`, a synchronous callback — because there's no suspension point available in a non-async context. The fix is wrapping the call in a `Task { }`, which starts a new asynchronous context inheriting the current actor and priority.

```swift
@IBAction func refreshTapped() {
    // Wrong: won't compile — can't 'await' in a sync function
    // let data = await fetchData()

    Task {
        let data = await fetchData()
        self.updateUI(with: data)
    }
}
```

The subtlety: a `Task` started this way is **detached from the caller's lifetime** by default — it keeps running even if the view that started it disappears, unless you explicitly store and cancel it (`onDisappear { task?.cancel() }`), which is its own common source of wasted work or, worse, a UI update firing after the screen is gone.

The oldest concurrency bug on the platform, still common in GCD-era code: a background-queue closure directly touches a `UILabel` or `@Published` property without hopping back to main.

```swift
DispatchQueue.global().async {
    let image = downloadImage(url)
    self.imageView.image = image   // wrong: UI touched off main
}
```

The symptom ranges from silent (the update just doesn't visibly apply reliably) to a crash in Debug builds with `-com.apple.CoreUI.MainThreadOnlyKit` style assertions, to actual visual corruption under contention. The fix under GCD is an explicit `DispatchQueue.main.async` wrap; under structured concurrency, marking the owning type `@MainActor` makes the compiler **reject** the mistake at compile time instead of only catching it at runtime — a meaningful safety upgrade, which is a strong argument for migrating UI-facing classes to `@MainActor` even in an otherwise GCD-based codebase.

A junior answer treats actors as 'the thing that makes concurrency safe.' A senior answer knows actors only serialize access to **their own** state — a **reentrancy** gap exists: between two `await` points inside the same actor method, another task can run against that same actor and observe or mutate state in a way that invalidates an assumption the first call made before its `await`.

```swift
actor Account {
    var balance = 100
    func withdraw(_ amount: Int) async {
        guard balance >= amount else { return }
        await logTransaction()      // suspension point: another withdraw can run here
        balance -= amount            // balance may have changed since the guard
    }
}
```

Two concurrent `withdraw` calls can both pass the guard before either decrements `balance`, because the `await` in between yields the actor to the other call. This is the concurrency equivalent of a classic race condition, just moved to a different mechanism — actors remove **data races** (simultaneous raw memory access) but not **logic races** across suspension points, and only careful ordering (re-check the invariant after the `await`, or avoid suspending mid-invariant) fixes it.
